#[doc("""
What each command says of itself when asked with `--help`.

A command that does not know `--help` is read as a command: `kalku mcp
--help` served MCP on stdin, and waited there for good. So the question is
answered before any command is read, and answered for every one of them.
""")]

#[doc("The commands that have something to say of themselves.")]
pub fn explained() : [String] = [
  "init",
  "run",
  "cast",
  "show",
  "merge",
  "propose-equivalent",
  "accept",
  "reject",
  "info",
  "serve",
  "mcp"
]

#[doc("The command a command line asks about, when it asks: `kalku <command> --help`, or `kalku help <command>`.")]
pub fn asked_about(argv: [String]) : Option[String] = match argv {
  ["help", cmd, ...] -> if contains(explained(), cmd) { Some(cmd) } else { None }
  [cmd, ...rest] -> if contains(explained(), cmd) and any(rest, is_help) { Some(cmd) } else { None }
  _ -> None
}

fn is_help(arg: String) : Bool = arg == "--help" or arg == "-h"

#[doc("What a command does, what it writes, and what it takes.")]
pub fn help_of(cmd: String) : String = match cmd {
  "init" -> INIT
  "run" -> RUN
  "cast" -> CAST
  "show" -> SHOW
  "merge" -> MERGE
  "propose-equivalent" -> PROPOSE
  "accept" -> ACCEPT
  "reject" -> REJECT
  "info" -> INFO
  "serve" -> SERVE
  "mcp" -> MCP
  _ -> ""
}

const INIT : String = """
usage: kalku init [dir] [--lang <name>] [-y]

Set a project up, so that what a run needs is kept with it: a configuration
the team shares, equivalents that are argued and recorded, a gate in CI.

It reads the project's own markers (`mix.exs`, `kai.toml`, `Cargo.toml`,
`pyproject.toml`, `pom.xml`), says what it found and what it will write,
and asks before writing:

  .kalku.toml      how a run is set up
  .kalku/summon    how this project's kalku is summoned
  the build file   for Elixir, one line in `deps`, named before it is added

Nothing that is already there is written over. A project does not have to
be set up to be measured: `kalku run <file>` measures it as it is and
writes nothing in it.

options:
  --lang <name>    the project's language, when init should not guess
  -y, --yes        do not ask before writing
"""

const RUN : String = """
usage: kalku run [files...] [--since <ref>] [--all] [--watch] [options]

Cast wekufe over those files and report the ones that survived, each with
its file, line, spell, diff and a hint. Nothing is written in the project's
working tree: wekufe are cast in the reni, under the temporary directory.

While it runs it says, on stderr, which phase it is in and how far the
casting has got, and names a cast that has gone on for 30 s or more: on a
terminal as one line drawn in place, elsewhere as a line per phase and a
count every 30 s. stdout carries the report alone. Every run keeps a log
in the reni, each note and every line a kalku wrote on stderr; its path is
the first thing the run says.

exit code: 0 when nothing blocks, 1 when a wekufe survived on a changed
line or the score is under what the project holds it to, 2 when the run
could not measure.

options:
  --limit <n>      cast at most n wekufe: 100 by default, 0 for all of them
                   (a run with --since casts the whole change unless told otherwise)
  --since <ref>    measure only the lines changed since that ref, and block
                   when a wekufe survives on one of them
  --all            measure every source file of the project, all of its wekufe
  --shard <i/n>    cast only part i of n: the same part on every machine
  --store-baseline store this run's score as the baseline `ratchet = true`
                   holds later runs of the whole project to
  --every-kill     with --all: cast each killed wekufe again without its killer
                   until no test left kills it, and name the tests that kill
                   nothing; slower by as many casts as there are kills
  --watch          run again every time a source or test file changes, until
                   interrupted; the exit code is the last run's
  --format <name>  human (the default), agent, json, or github
  -v, --verbose    say everything the run is doing, a line per wekufe
  -q, --quiet      say nothing until the report
"""

const CAST : String = """
usage: kalku cast <wekufe>... [--format <name>] [-v | -q]

Re-cast these wekufe, after a test was written for them. A wekufe is named
as a run named it. What comes back is what a run reports, for these alone.

options:
  --format <name>  human (the default), agent, json, or github
  -v, --verbose    say everything the run is doing, a line per wekufe
  -q, --quiet      say nothing until the report
"""

const MERGE : String = """
usage: kalku merge <report.json>... [--format <name>] [--store-baseline]

Combine the reports of a run split across jobs into the report of the
whole project. Each report is what `kalku run --all --shard i/n --format
json` wrote; the merge is held to `score.threshold` and to the ratchet's
baseline as a run of the whole project is.

A merge is refused, with exit 2, unless the reports are every part of one
plan exactly once: shards of the same count, written by this kalku, from
the same sites at the same commit, each having measured what it owned.

exit code: 0 when nothing blocks, 1 when the score is under what the
project holds it to, 2 when the reports do not make the whole project.

options:
  --format <name>  human (the default), agent, json, or github
  --store-baseline store the merged score as the baseline `ratchet = true`
                   holds later runs of the whole project to
"""

const SHOW : String = """
usage: kalku show <wekufe> [--format <name>]

One wekufe in full, from what the last runs kept: its site, the diff, the
tests that judged it, a hint, and what became of it run after run. Nothing
is cast.

options:
  --format <name>  human (the default) or json
"""

const PROPOSE : String = """
usage: kalku propose-equivalent <wekufe> --reason <why>

Argue that a survivor cannot be told apart from the original. The argument
is written to `.kalku/equivalent.proposed`, where a person reads it; it
suppresses nothing until `kalku accept` takes it. Only a survivor can be
proposed.

options:
  --reason <why>   why no test could tell the wekufe from the original
"""

const ACCEPT : String = """
usage: kalku accept <wekufe> [--reason <why>]

Take a proposed equivalent into `.kalku/equivalent`, which every run reads:
the wekufe is counted as equivalent from then on, with the reason beside
it. One wekufe at a time, and only one that was proposed.

options:
  --reason <why>   what convinced you, recorded beside what was argued
"""

const REJECT : String = """
usage: kalku reject <wekufe> [--reason <why>]

Turn a proposed equivalent down. It leaves `.kalku/equivalent.proposed`
for `.kalku/equivalent.rejected`, and the wekufe is a survivor that wants
a test again.

options:
  --reason <why>   why the argument does not hold
"""

const INFO : String = """
usage: kalku info [topic] [--json] [--snippet]

What kalku knows about spells, outcomes, formats and agents. Without a
topic, the list of them.

options:
  --json           print the topic as JSON
  --snippet        with `agents`: only the lines to paste into CLAUDE.md
"""

const SERVE : String = """
usage: kalku serve

Answer the client protocol on a local socket, and keep the kalku of each
project warm between runs. The socket is a file only its owner can open,
under `$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR` or the temporary directory; nothing listens on
the network. It runs until it is interrupted.
"""

const MCP : String = """
usage: kalku mcp

Serve the agent tools to an MCP host, over stdio: `kalku_run`,
`kalku_cast`, `kalku_show` and `kalku_propose_equivalent`. It is started
by the host, not by hand: it reads JSON-RPC on stdin until the host goes
away. A host speaks first; if nothing arrives within 10 seconds, it says so
and exits 2. In Claude Code:

  claude mcp add kalku -- kalku mcp
"""
